  "narrative": "The central dataset-level associations are now well supported and validated: KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss are associated with higher dependency_composite values, meaning weaker composite dependency, while SMARCA4 loss and PTEN loss are associated with lower, more negative dependency_composite values, meaning stronger composite dependency. The next scientific priority is not to re-confirm these main effects, but to test how stable they are under stringent residual-co-mutation exclusion and meaningful contexts. I register full-central-loss exclusion contexts for KEAP1 and SMARCA4: high-quality screen, adenocarcinoma subtype, and the dominant Avana CRISPR library. These ask whether the large KEAP1 and SMARCA4 associations are robust when the other three major central losses are removed and when the analysis is restricted to higher-quality screens, a dominant lineage, or a major library. If the estimates remain far above the 0.15-unit threshold, context robustness is supported. If they attenuate or become uncertain, the central effects remain accepted average within-dataset associations but should be interpreted as context-sensitive. These are associations only, not causal effects, single-gene knockout claims, or external generalizability claims.\n\nThe most important unresolved predictor cluster is CDKN2A and MTAP. CDKN2A is repeatedly near but not clearly above the threshold, while MTAP has not yet been resolved under strong exclusion. I register CDKN2A loss with full central-loss exclusion plus high-quality screens, CDKN2A loss with MTAP-intact full central exclusion, MTAP loss with CDKN2A-intact full central exclusion, and a CDKN2A-by-MTAP interaction under full central exclusion. This design distinguishes several alternatives: an independent but modest CDKN2A association, an MTAP co-deletion proxy, a stronger MTAP association, or co-deletion synergy. If CDKN2A remains above threshold only in some contexts, it should be treated cautiously as near-threshold; if mutual-exclusion contrasts are clean and large, it may warrant acceptance or validation.\n\nFor TP53 and PTEN, I also register adenocarcinoma contexts under full central-loss exclusion and STK11-intact eligibility. STK11 loss was null in the registered negative direction, but controlling it tests whether the accepted TP53 and PTEN associations remain coherent after removing a common tumor-suppressor-loss context. If these context contrasts pass, the central associations are more robust across supplied-dataset contexts. If they do not, the main effects remain supported overall but with clearer context-dependence. All proposals remain in-dataset association tests; they do not establish mechanism, causality, or generalizable biological meaning.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 5 explore: Central accepted landscape: KEAP1 loss positive/weaker dependency, SMARCA4 loss negative/stronger dependency, TP53 loss positive/weaker dependency, PTEN loss negative/stronger dependency; all main effects validated. Focus now: context robustness and CDKN2A/MTAP resolution. Registered full-central-loss exclusion contexts for KEAP1/SMARCA4 with high-quality screens, adenocarcinoma, and Avana library. Registered CDKN2A high-quality full central exclusion, CDKN2A MTAP-intact full central exclusion, MTAP CDKN2A-intact full central exclusion, and CDKN2A*MTAP interaction full central exclusion. Registered TP53 and PTEN adenocarcinoma full-central STK11-intact refinements to test additional context coherence. Keep CDKN2A unresolved unless evidence crosses 0.15 with reasonable uncertainty in MTAP/central-controlled contrasts. Existing untested H76/H77/H86/H87/H88 and broad predictors remain useful if cell counts permit. Do not request validation unless a new context or CDKN2A/MTAP contrast becomes decisive."
