onger-dependency direction, RB1 loss in the claimed weaker-dependency direction, SMAD4 loss, APC mutation, BRAF V600E, the constructed research markers, MTAP in central-loss-excluded contexts, ERBB2 amplification, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, media serum difference, CRISPR library offset, and matched omics-availability offset.\n\nThe major data-driven refinement is MSI-high. The originally registered positive-direction claims were rejected because the observed signed estimates were large and negative. The corresponding reverse claims now have the appropriately signed evidence. H120 gives a signed overall MSI-high effect of +0.792, interval +0.676 to +0.907, and H121 gives a signed effect of +0.754, interval +0.683 to +0.825, after excluding TP53, SMARCA4, KEAP1, and PTEN loss. Both are far beyond the threshold in the reverse direction. I therefore accept and keep active H120 and H121: MSI-high cells have substantially lower, more negative dependency_composite values, meaning stronger composite dependency, and this pattern is not explained by the four central losses. This is still a supplied-dataset association, not a causal effect, not a single-gene knockout measurement, and not a claim of external generalizability. The next priority is to understand where the MSI-high effect is robust and whether it is modified by lineage subtype, mutation burden, high-quality screens, or co-occurrence with TP53 loss. Proposed analyses focus on these contexts, with formal interactions to distinguish a broadly present association from genuine effect modification.",
