  "narrative": "The strongest current conclusions are four central loss associations and a large reversed MSI-high association. KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss are associated with higher, less negative dependency_composite values, meaning weaker composite dependency, whereas SMARCA4 loss and PTEN loss are associated with lower, more negative values, meaning stronger composite dependency. The new MSI-high signal is far above the 0.15-unit threshold in the stronger-dependency direction and is not removed by excluding the four central losses. The next priority is therefore not to rediscover these main effects but to ask where the MSI-high association is robust and whether it modifies the central landscape.\n\nI register three MSI-high robustness proposals. First, central-loss-intact high-quality-screen MSI-high tests whether the reversed effect persists when both major co-driver losses and weaker-screen quality are removed. Second, central-loss-intact high mutation burden tests whether the association is still present among cells with elevated mutation burden, the context most likely to confound MSI-high status. Third, a formal interaction with the central-loss-intact subgroup tests whether the MSI-high association is materially different in cells lacking central losses versus cells with at least one central loss. These tests distinguish a broadly present MSI-high association from one limited to a small technical or mutational context.\n\nI also register reciprocal stratification of the accepted central predictors by MSI status. Formal interactions ask whether the KEAP1, SMARCA4, and TP53 effects differ between MSI-high and MSI-negative cells. Because MSI-high cells may have distinct biology, these interactions ask whether the central dependency landscape is homogeneous or context-dependent. Direct within-MSI-high mean differences are registered for KEAP1, SMARCA4, and TP53 to estimate whether each central effect remains present in MSI-high cells, although these cells are small and the intervals may be wide. PTEN is too rare within MSI-high for a reliable positive-stratum test, so I register the larger MSI-negative context instead; this tests whether PTEN loss remains associated with stronger dependency when the small but strong MSI-high subgroup is excluded. All proposals remain supplied-dataset associations, not causal effects, single-gene knockout claims, or claims about external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 7 explore update: accepted within-dataset landscape is KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss -> higher/less negative dependency_composite (weaker dependency); SMARCA4 loss and PTEN loss -> lower/more negative dependency_composite (stronger dependency). MSI-high accepted reverse: lower/more negative dependency_composite (stronger dependency), overall signed +0.792 and central-loss-intact signed +0.754. CDKN2A remains unresolved/closed low priority; MTAP and many secondary/technical predictors unsupported.\n\nNew priority is MSI-high robustness and cross-modification. Registered: MSI central-loss-intact high-quality screens; MSI central-loss-intact high mutation burden; MSI interaction with central-loss-intact status. Also registered central-loss-by-MSI interactions for KEAP1, SMARCA4, TP53; direct within MSI-high estimates for KEAP1, SMARCA4, TP53; and PTEN in MSI-negative cells to test whether PTEN association survives excluding the small MSI-high subgroup. PTEN within MSI-high avoided due likely cell-count constraint. If MSI effect remains strong across high-quality/high-burden/central-intact contexts, favor a broadly present MSI-high association; if interactions are large, favor context-specific modification. Continue treating all findings as supplied-dataset associations only."
