  "narrative": "No assessments are due. The accepted central landscape is now well characterized in the supplied dataset, so these new proposals are deliberately framed as context-boundary and technical-robustness tests rather than rediscovery of main effects. I first test whether KEAP1 loss, SMARCA4 loss, and TP53 loss retain their accepted associations within MSI-negative, high chromosomal-instability cells, defined by aneuploidy_score in the upper quartile. The MSI-negative restriction removes the very strong MSI-high contrast, while high aneuploidy focuses on a biologically relevant chromosomal-destabilized context. The expected raw exposed-minus-comparator signs are positive for KEAP1 and TP53, meaning higher/less negative dependency_composite and weaker dependency, and negative for SMARCA4, meaning lower/more negative dependency_composite and stronger dependency. Plausible alternatives are attenuation, absence, or opposite modification if these associations are concentrated outside high-aneuploidy cells or if chromosomal instability dominates the composite score. I next test high stemness and high EMT contexts within the same MSI-negative population. KEAP1 and SMARCA4 are examined among high-stemness cells because dedifferentiation/chromatin states could amplify or bypass the accepted associations. SMARCA4 and PTEN loss are examined among high-EMT cells because EMT is a plausible boundary condition for lineage-dependent and PTEN-associated dependency patterns. Expected raw signs remain those of the accepted main effects: positive for KEAP1 and negative for SMARCA4/PTEN, although attenuation or no context difference is plausible if these phenotypes reflect overall screen state rather than effect-specific biology. For MSI-high, I test median-split high SLFN11 expression and median-split high IFN-gamma context. SLFN11 is relevant because it may mark DNA-damage-response states, while IFN-gamma may mark immune-active contexts; both could strengthen, weaken, or leave unchanged the accepted strong MSI-high stronger-dependency association. I use median splits rather than upper quartiles for these MSI-exposed contexts to maintain adequate cell counts in MSI-high comparison cells. Finally, I register two screen_batch_a context tests for TP53 loss and MSI-high as technical homogeneity checks. These do not assert a biological batch direction; they test whether the accepted associations remain visible inside a single batch. Expected raw signs are the accepted main-effect directions, but an unresolved or null result could reflect sampling, batch-specific screen variation, or a true context limitation. All proposed comparisons are within-supplied-dataset associations under explicit eligibility and subgroup definitions; they are not causal claims, single-gene knockout measurements, or externally generalizable biological claims. If analysis capacity is limited, prioritize the three high-aneuploidy MSI-negative central-loss tests, then PTEN high-EMT, then MSI-SLFN11/IFN and batch-context checks."
