  "narrative": "The new validation resolves the boundary uncertainty for TP53 loss in lower-quality screens and confirms the core TP53 association. H170, TP53 loss in MSI-negative cells with lower screen separation, had a discovery signed estimate of +0.352 with lower bound +0.133, below the 0.15-unit meaningful-effect threshold. The independent validation signed estimate is +0.583 with lower bound +0.322, well above threshold and excluding both null and threshold-level uncertainty. I accept H170 and close it. This means that, within this supplied dataset, TP53 loss is associated with a higher, less negative dependency_composite value, i.e. weaker composite dependency, even in the lower screen-quality stratum. H145 remains a narrow, high-quality screen, all-loss-absent MSI-negative context for PTEN loss. Its discovery signed estimate was +0.255 with a 95% lower bound of +0.157, but the independent validation signed estimate was +0.255 with lower bound +0.094, which does not clear the 0.15-unit threshold under the stringent independent interval. I keep H145 unresolved and close the investigation: the point estimates are directionally supportive, but the validation interval leaves uncertainty about whether this very narrow stratum exceeds the meaningful-effect threshold. This does not undermine broader accepted PTEN associations, including H37, H49, H70, H149, and related context tests. H81, the KEAP1-loss association modified by PTEN-positive status in the claimed positive direction, was not supported. Discovery gave a small positive signed estimate of +0.145 and validation gave +0.093, with validation interval crossing null. I reject H81 and close it. This argues against a meaningful positive interaction between KEAP1 and PTEN for the claimed direction; it does not establish exact absence of all interaction, but the available evidence does not support a threshold-level KEAP1-by-PTEN modification. Overall, the accepted within-dataset landscape remains robust: KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss are associated with higher, less negative dependency_composite scores, i.e. weaker dependency, whereas SMARCA4 loss, MSI-high status, and PTEN loss are associated with lower, more negative scores, i.e. stronger dependency. KEAP1, SMARCA4, TP53, and MSI-high effects are large and validated in several contexts; PTEN effects are moderate and generally around +0.22 to +0.27 signed units, with some near-threshold contexts remaining unresolved. Rejected negative findings include KRAS mutation, MYC amplification, EGFR amplification, PIK3CA mutation, ARID1A loss, RB1 loss, ERBB2 amplification, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, APC mutation, BRAF V600E, many MTAP contexts, research markers D/E/F, media serum contrast, matched omics availability, Avana library as a main effect, and batch_a versus batch_b main effect. Interaction claims are mostly unsupported or unresolved. The most informative next work should avoid additional tiny co-loss strata and instead focus on broad near-threshold main-effect contexts and technical coherence, such as PTEN in KEAP1-intact or SMARCA4-intact contexts, MSI-high with IFN-related or SLFN11-related contexts, and broad batch/library robustness tests if they have sufficient cell counts. All conclusions remain supplied-dataset associations under explicit contrasts, not causal effects, single-gene knockout claims, or externally generalizable biological claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 10 synthesis update. H170 accepted and closed: TP53 loss in lower-quality MSI-negative screen cells is validated at +0.583 signed units, lower bound +0.322. H145 kept unresolved and closed: strict high-quality, KEAP1/SMARCA4/TP53/MSI-negative PTEN context has point estimate +0.255 but independent validation lower bound +0.094 below threshold; uncertainty remains only in this narrow stratum. H81 rejected and closed: KEAP1-by-PTEN positive interaction estimates are small, +0.145 discovery and +0.093 validation, and validation interval crosses null; no threshold-level interaction in claimed direction. Supported within-dataset predictor landscape: KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency, large validated effect around +0.82; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency, large validated effect around +0.83 signed; TP53 loss -> weaker dependency, around +0.40 and now validated also in lower-quality screens; MSI-high -> stronger dependency, around +0.75 to +0.80; PTEN loss -> stronger dependency, moderate, accepted broader and several context tests around +0.22 to +0.27. Negative/rejected main effects: KRAS, MYC, EGFR, PIK3CA, ARID1A, RB1, ERBB2, BRCA2, NF1, APC, BRAF, research markers D/E/F, media serum, matched omics, Avana main effect, and batch_a versus batch_b main effect. MTAP generally unsupported in tested contexts. CDKN2A is borderline and not treated as robust accepted predictor. Unresolved areas: narrow PTEN high-quality all-loss-absent stratum, several near-threshold PTEN co-loss or context cells, many interactions including H80 borderline, small co-loss strata, and some technical/context modifiers. Next best analyses: broad near-threshold main-effect tests rather than tiny strata. Propose a broader PTEN loss test in MSI-negative, TP53-intact, SMARCA4-intact cells without excluding KEAP1 loss, motivated by H145 uncertainty. Continue with registered broad active tests such as PTEN KEAP1-intact or SMARCA4-intact contexts, MSI-high IFN-gamma or SLFN11 contexts, and broad batch/library robustness tests if adequately powered. Use remaining validation capacity, if available, for broad near-threshold discoveries rather than additional tiny strata. Do not interpret accepted claims as causal, single-gene knockout, or externally generalizable."
