  "narrative": "The twelve new discovery contrasts strengthen the central supplied-dataset predictor landscape while clarifying several technical and near-threshold caveats. I use the ledger's signed-direction convention: for positive-direction KEAP1 and TP53 claims, a positive signed estimate means higher/less negative dependency_composite, i.e. weaker composite dependency; for negative-direction SMARCA4, MSI-high, PTEN, and CDKN2A claims, a positive signed estimate means lower/more negative scores, i.e. stronger composite dependency. H178, H179, and H180 test whether KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss, and TP53-loss contrasts persist among cells with high measured Cas9 activity (cas9_activity_pct >= 88.9). All three remain above the 0.15-unit threshold, with H178 signed +0.735 (lower +0.594), H179 signed +0.691 (lower +0.536), and H180 signed +0.396 (lower +0.198). I accept all three, although H180 is more borderline and Cas9-activity data are not complete, so this is descriptive robustness in a measured high-Cas9 subset rather than a causal or generalizable perturbation-quality claim. H47 and H48 show that TP53-loss association also persists in adenocarcinoma and higher screen-quality contexts, with signed estimates +0.414 and +0.462 and lower bounds well above threshold; both are accepted and closed. H158 shows that the MSI-high stronger-dependency association persists in the median-or-high IFN-gamma signature stratum, signed +0.811 (lower +0.677), and is accepted. H177 is the most informative PTEN result: in a broad TP53-intact, SMARCA4-intact, MSI-negative context, the signed PTEN-loss estimate is +0.255 with lower bound +0.168, just clearing the threshold. I provisionally accept it because the 95% discovery interval clears the practical threshold, but I keep H177 active and request independent validation because it is near the threshold and could materially affect confidence in a broad PTEN context without disturbing the broader accepted PTEN landscape. H182, H183, and H112 are directionally supportive PTEN contrasts with point estimates above threshold, but their lower bounds are far below 0.15 (H182 +0.066, H183 +0.047, H112 +0.065), so they remain unresolved and deferred; independent validation is unlikely to resolve them under stringent independent intervals compared with H177. H181 is a broad CDKN2A test in MSI-negative, TP53-intact cells. Its signed estimate is +0.146, below the threshold, with lower bound +0.073 and upper bound +0.220. Because the interval still crosses the threshold, I do not reject exact absence, but I leave it unresolved and defer further CDKN2A work: across several adequately powered contexts, CDKN2A has not produced a convincing threshold-supported effect. This prompted reassessment of prior borderline accepted CDKN2A and PTEN claims: H88 remains unresolved and closed because its validation point estimate is below threshold and its independent interval is broad, and H138 is moved from accepted to unresolved because its lower bound (+0.138) does not clear the threshold despite a supportive point estimate. H20, the SMARCA4-by-KEAP1 interaction, is rejected for the claimed negative interaction: the signed interval [-0.167, +0.121] excludes a meaningful negative interaction in the hypothesized direction and centers near null. Overall, multiplicity and selection over subgroups remain relevant. These are unadjusted descriptive contrasts, not causal effects, single-gene knockout mechanisms, or external generalizations. The evidence still supports a robust within-dataset landscape: KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss are associated with weaker composite dependency (less negative scores), whereas SMARCA4 loss, MSI-high status, and PTEN loss are associated with stronger composite dependency (more negative scores).",
