  "narrative": "H170 validation deadline: TP53-loss versus TP53-intact among MSI-negative cells with lower-quality screens. The claim direction is positive, so positive signed estimates mean higher/less negative dependency_composite, i.e. weaker composite dependency. Discovery gave signed +0.352 with 95% lower bound +0.133, while independent validation gave signed +0.583 with stringent lower bound +0.322. The validation lower bound exceeds the 0.15-unit meaningful-effect threshold, so I accept and close H170. This strengthens the TP53-loss weaker-dependency landscape in the supplied dataset and shows that it is not confined to higher-quality screens within MSI-negative cells.\n\nCurrent scientific synthesis. The robust within-dataset associations are: KEAP1 loss, signed positive estimates about +0.73 to +0.93, meaning weaker composite dependency; TP53 loss, signed about +0.36 to +0.58, meaning weaker composite dependency; SMARCA4 loss, signed positive about +0.62 to +0.93 under negative-direction claims, meaning stronger composite dependency; and MSI-high status, signed about +0.74 to +0.86, meaning stronger composite dependency. These large effects persist across many contexts and several received independent validation. PTEN loss is supported as a moderate stronger-dependency association in the supplied dataset: accepted broad and contextual contrasts include point estimates roughly +0.20 to +0.38, and the narrow clean context H149 has independent validation with lower bound just clearing threshold (+0.271; lower +0.151). However, PTEN is less uniform and less precisely bounded than the large effects. Several PTEN strata remain unresolved, deferred, or threshold-sensitive, including the broader H177 context after validation failure to clear the stringent threshold, high-TMB and Cas9-quality boundaries, and some predictor contexts. Therefore PTEN should be reported as real in this dataset but context-limited and not uniformly threshold-cleared. CDKN2A is not convincingly meaningful; its point estimates cluster near or below threshold and some validation intervals are weak. Many measured predictors show no meaningful association: KRAS mutation, MYC amplification, EGFR amplification, PIK3CA mutation, ARID1A loss, RB1 loss, ERBB2 amplification, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, APC mutation, BRAF V600E, media-serum contrast, matched-omics availability, Avana library main effect, and batch_a versus batch_b main effect. Research markers D/E/F and the three research signatures did not establish threshold-level main-effect or interaction findings under tested contrasts. Interaction claims mostly remain rejected, unresolved, or underpowered; no robust modifier or epistasis effect is established. All conclusions are descriptive associations in the supplied dataset, not causal effects, single-gene knockout mechanistic claims, or externally generalizable claims. The next most informative analyses are targeted PTEN-loss contrasts, especially the registered high-Cas9/TP53-intact and low-Cas9/TP53-intact comparisons plus the newly proposed SMARCA4-intact high-Cas9 and low-Cas9 PTEN contrasts. These test whether the moderate PTEN signal survives the most relevant technical-quality and strong-predictor strata. Further tiny co-loss strata or low-information signature interactions are lower priority unless cell sizes and scientific rationale are stronger.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 12 synthesis update. H170/R132 confirms TP53-loss weaker composite dependency in MSI-negative lower-quality screens with validation lower bound +0.322; accept and close. Validation capacity remains exhausted. Current accepted landscape is dominated by large KEAP1-loss and SMARCA4-loss associations and by TP53-loss and MSI-high associations, all descriptive supplied-dataset contrasts. PTEN loss remains moderately supported but context-sensitive: validated in a clean narrow context (H149/R119), accepted in several contexts, but not independently threshold-cleared in broader H177 and many boundary strata. CDKN2A remains weak/uncertain. Interactions are not robustly supported. Next analysis priority: resolve PTEN within Cas9-activity strata and remove or condition on strong competing effects, especially SMARCA4-loss and TP53-loss. Proposed new PTEN contrasts: high Cas9 with SMARCA4-intact, and low Cas9 with SMARCA4-intact, refining H190 and H198. Existing H204/H205 (low and high Cas9 TP53-intact PTEN) should remain active. If these threshold-clear, PTEN can be described as robust moderate predictor under stronger-quality or TP53-intact/SMARCA4-intact contexts; if not, keep PTEN accepted only as broad/moderate but context-limited. Avoid additional low-powered interactions unless interval evidence can exclude threshold-level uncertainty."
}

